<?xml version="1.0"?>
<model>
  <name>hillstar_arm_clearance_stand</name>
  <version>1.0</version>
  <sdf version='1.9'>model.sdf</sdf>

  <author>
   <name>Aabhash Bhandari</name>
  </author>

  <description>
    Static bench stand for hillstar_continuum_arm_gripper: two parallel
    rails sized/spaced to match hillstar_base's own landing skids (see
    hillstar_base/model.sdf's collision_leg_left/right comment - skids span
    x[-0.145,0.145] at y=+/-0.1715), raised on 4 corner legs to a 0.55m
    platform height. The vehicle's landing skids rest on the rails; the
    0.313m open gap between the rails, and open space beyond their ends,
    is left completely clear all the way down to true ground so the
    continuum arm + gripper can hang in its neutral rest pose without
    touching anything.

    Rationale: hillstar_continuum_arm_gripper's arm mount sits at
    base_link_z-0.015, hangs 0.40m along -Z, and the gripper's own
    grasp-center reach adds another 0.08533m past the arm tip - about
    0.485m below the mount, i.e. ~0.29m below the skids' own foot level
    (skids bottom out only 0.21m below base_link). On flat ground the arm
    has nowhere to go: it buckles/curls sideways under the vehicle instead
    of hanging straight, and does not spring back on takeoff, causing the
    instability this stand exists to avoid. At 0.55m platform height the
    arm+gripper tip clears true ground by roughly 0.26m even resting in a
    relaxed (not perfectly straight) neutral shape.

    Not physically load-path-realistic (real skids do not usually stand on
    a fixture like this) - purely a simulation/bench-test aid so the soft
    arm's own dynamics can be exercised without a ground-contact artifact.
  </description>
</model>
